WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.073
Welcome to our secondary education
professional learning

2
00:00:08.073 --> 00:00:08.789
workshop.

3
00:00:08.789 --> 00:00:12.497
Today we're going to explore Autistic
experience and what it

4
00:00:12.497 --> 00:00:16.298
means for access, belonging and
partnership in a secondary

5
00:00:16.298 --> 00:00:16.853
school.

6
00:00:16.853 --> 00:00:20.226
Keep the students you work with at the
centre of the day.

7
00:00:20.226 --> 00:00:23.911
We'll build our understanding, look
closely at everyday school

8
00:00:23.911 --> 00:00:27.169
routines and consider what we can change
in the environment, the

9
00:00:27.169 --> 00:00:30.045
task and the support we offer.

10
00:00:30.045 --> 00:00:33.869
Our starting point is curiosity. We are
here to understand

11
00:00:33.869 --> 00:00:36.746
students and make participation more
workable.

12
00:00:36.884 --> 00:00:40.523
We value students as they are and support
their participation

13
00:00:40.523 --> 00:00:41.644
and belonging.

14
00:00:41.644 --> 00:00:45.710
You don't need to arrive with all the
answers. Bring your

15
00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:48.644
questions, your experience and a
willingness to look again.

16
00:00:51.121 --> 00:00:56.043
I acknowledge the Traditional Custodians
of the lands on which we are learning

17
00:00:56.044 --> 00:01:01.158
today, and pay my respects to Elders past
and present. I recognise the continuing

18
00:01:01.159 --> 00:01:05.698
connections of Aboriginal and Torres
Strait Islander peoples to Country,

19
00:01:05.699 --> 00:01:10.493
culture and community. I extend that
respect to Aboriginal and Torres Strait

20
00:01:10.494 --> 00:01:13.689
Islander people taking part in this
learning today.

21
00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:19.780
Our Statement of Reconciliation is in your
workbook for you to read.

22
00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:22.920
For the sake of this demonstration, I'll
read it aloud.

23
00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:26.880
We at Positive Partnerships value and
recognise the diversity of

24
00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:30.320
Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander
cultures and heritage

25
00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:34.520
and their proud part in Australia’s
national identity.

26
00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:38.530
We acknowledge and continue to learn about
Australia’s colonial

27
00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:43.640
past and the historic and ongoing impacts
on Australia’s First Nations people.

28
00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:48.270
We believe in and strongly support the
Truth-telling of

29
00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:50.140
Australia’s colonial history

30
00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:53.850
and in moving forward as a nation we must
encourage open,

31
00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:58.280
honest dialogue and build relationships
built on respect and trust.

32
00:02:00.681 --> 00:02:05.073
First Nations people are advised that this
workshop may contain images and

33
00:02:05.074 --> 00:02:07.599
voices of people who are no longer with
us.

34
00:02:09.601 --> 00:02:14.901
Everyone is welcome here, and you are
welcome to be authentically yourself. Use

35
00:02:14.902 --> 00:02:20.066
the strategies that help you take part.
You can move, change position, write,

36
00:02:20.067 --> 00:02:24.891
speak, listen or pass. If there is
something the delivery team can do to

37
00:02:24.892 --> 00:02:30.192
make participation more accessible, let us
know. Let's begin by getting to know

38
00:02:30.193 --> 00:02:35.425
the people around us. At your table, share
your first name, your role, and one

39
00:02:35.426 --> 00:02:40.183
hope you have for today. Keep the
introduction brief so everyone has an

40
00:02:40.184 --> 00:02:45.619
opportunity. You're also welcome simply to
listen. There is no need to disclose a

41
00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:50.785
diagnosis or share private information
about yourself, a student or a school.

42
00:02:50.786 --> 00:02:55.950
What would make this learning useful in
your work? Take a moment to introduce

43
00:02:55.951 --> 00:02:56.968
yourselves now.

44
00:03:05.001 --> 00:03:09.456
Let's come back together. Keep that hope
in mind as we go. There will be

45
00:03:09.457 --> 00:03:14.540
opportunities to connect the content with
sensory experience, thinking, the school

46
00:03:14.541 --> 00:03:18.996
day, learning design and partnership. Your
questions are useful starting

47
00:03:18.997 --> 00:03:22.321
points, even when they don't have an
immediate answer.

48
00:03:23.841 --> 00:03:27.604
Before we get into the content, take a
moment to check the practical

49
00:03:27.605 --> 00:03:32.154
arrangements for your setting: the exits,
bathroom facilities, tea and coffee, and

50
00:03:32.155 --> 00:03:36.536
any quiet or lower-sensory space that is
available. Keep your Participant Guide

51
00:03:36.537 --> 00:03:40.917
and something to write with nearby. We'll
use the guide throughout the day, and

52
00:03:40.918 --> 00:03:45.074
there will be time to provide feedback
through the evaluation. You can also

53
00:03:45.075 --> 00:03:49.343
write questions down if speaking to the
group isn't your preferred way to ask

54
00:03:49.344 --> 00:03:53.725
them. Please be considerate with phone
sounds and calls, and follow the venue's

55
00:03:53.726 --> 00:03:58.107
arrangements for no smoking or vaping.
Attend to your own needs as we work. You

56
00:03:58.108 --> 00:04:02.151
don't need to wait for a scheduled break
to use a strategy that helps you

57
00:04:02.152 --> 00:04:05.913
participate. Confirm any local
arrangements with your delivery team.

58
00:04:07.441 --> 00:04:12.214
Turn to page four of your Participant
Guide. Bring two or three students to

59
00:04:12.215 --> 00:04:17.181
mind. Choose students whose experiences
differ in some way: perhaps their age,

60
00:04:17.182 --> 00:04:22.149
how they communicate, or what different
parts of school are like for them. Use

61
00:04:22.150 --> 00:04:26.665
initials or a role description in your
notes. We won't need identifying

62
00:04:26.666 --> 00:04:31.761
information. Begin with the whole person.
What interests them? What strengths do

63
00:04:31.762 --> 00:04:36.471
you notice? How do they connect with
people or with the things they enjoy?

64
00:04:36.472 --> 00:04:41.438
Then ask yourself: what am I here to
understand more deeply about their school

65
00:04:41.439 --> 00:04:46.148
experience? Keep that as an open question,
rather than deciding the answer

66
00:04:46.149 --> 00:04:50.793
now. This is private thinking first.
Sharing is optional. We'll return to

67
00:04:50.794 --> 00:04:55.696
these same students when we map the school
day, consider learning design, and

68
00:04:55.697 --> 00:05:00.792
reflect on what to carry forward. Give
yourself a moment to choose your students

69
00:05:00.793 --> 00:05:02.146
and make a few notes.

70
00:05:10.161 --> 00:05:14.883
Keep those notes available. You don't need
a complete picture yet. The gaps in

71
00:05:14.884 --> 00:05:19.238
what we know are part of the work. As we
move through the workshop, keep

72
00:05:19.239 --> 00:05:23.654
distinguishing what a student has
communicated, what you've observed, and

73
00:05:23.655 --> 00:05:26.412
what you'd still like to understand with
them.

74
00:05:27.921 --> 00:05:32.677
We're going to begin with student voices.
Listen to each speaker's own account, and

75
00:05:32.678 --> 00:05:37.261
allow it to stand on its own terms. You
don't need to diagnose, compare speakers

76
00:05:37.262 --> 00:05:41.554
with students you know, or reduce the
video to one lesson. Just notice what

77
00:05:41.555 --> 00:05:45.906
stays with you, and what you want to
understand more deeply. We'll have time

78
00:05:45.907 --> 00:05:47.470
to reflect after the video.

79
00:08:09.881 --> 00:08:13.781
Take a moment before moving into
discussion. Keep the speakers' own words

80
00:08:13.782 --> 00:08:16.380
and experiences at the centre of your
reflection.

81
00:08:19.401 --> 00:08:24.290
Stay on page four of your Participant
Guide. What did you notice? What has

82
00:08:24.291 --> 00:08:29.314
stayed with you? What would you like to
understand more deeply? Begin with a

83
00:08:29.315 --> 00:08:34.606
little private writing. Anchor your notes
in what the speakers said, rather than

84
00:08:34.607 --> 00:08:39.430
making an interpretation from their
appearance or behaviour. Then, if you

85
00:08:39.431 --> 00:08:44.387
wish, share one reflection with someone
nearby. Try to leave each speaker's

86
00:08:44.388 --> 00:08:49.612
account intact. We don't need to reach
agreement about a single message, and we

87
00:08:49.613 --> 00:08:54.502
don't need to compare a speaker with one
particular child. Make room for a

88
00:08:54.503 --> 00:08:59.459
question that remains open as well as
something that feels clear. Take some

89
00:08:59.460 --> 00:09:01.335
time with those prompts now.

90
00:09:09.361 --> 00:09:14.177
Let's return. Keep one question from that
reflection where you can see it. We will

91
00:09:14.178 --> 00:09:18.340
come back to sensory experience, thinking,
communication and the school

92
00:09:18.341 --> 00:09:22.860
day. Some questions may need further
conversation through the program or your

93
00:09:22.861 --> 00:09:27.261
local channels. Listening carefully
includes recognising what we still need

94
00:09:27.262 --> 00:09:27.796
to learn.

95
00:09:29.320 --> 00:09:33.968
Let's take a moment with the language on
this slide, because it gives us a shared

96
00:09:33.968 --> 00:09:38.208
starting point. Neurodiversity is the
whole picture. It includes everyone.

97
00:09:38.208 --> 00:09:42.914
Neurotypical and neurodivergent describe
brain patterns within that diversity, and

98
00:09:42.914 --> 00:09:47.271
autism is one expression of it. Here,
neurotypical describes the more common

99
00:09:47.271 --> 00:09:51.744
brain patterns. Neurodivergent describes
brain patterns that differ from those

100
00:09:51.744 --> 00:09:56.160
most common patterns. So both sit within
the wider picture of neurodiversity.

101
00:09:56.160 --> 00:10:00.517
Keeping those ideas clear helps us use the
language consistently through the

102
00:10:00.517 --> 00:10:04.990
day. Now, hold in mind the students you
were thinking about earlier. The words

103
00:10:04.990 --> 00:10:09.521
can help us ask better questions about
their experience, but they won't tell us

104
00:10:09.521 --> 00:10:13.530
everything about any one of them. We still
need to know that student's

105
00:10:13.530 --> 00:10:18.003
interests, their ways of communicating,
and what different parts of school are

106
00:10:18.003 --> 00:10:22.418
like for them. We'll also respect the
language people use for themselves. The

107
00:10:22.418 --> 00:10:26.834
purpose here is to support dignity and
curiosity. As we go, keep asking: does

108
00:10:26.834 --> 00:10:30.201
this language help me understand the
person in front of me?

109
00:10:31.721 --> 00:10:36.119
That brings us to what it means to be
neurodiversity affirming. The starting

110
00:10:36.120 --> 00:10:40.224
point is that differences are not
deficits. There are different ways of

111
00:10:40.225 --> 00:10:44.799
knowing, doing, being and belonging, and
everyone has strengths and experiences

112
00:10:44.800 --> 00:10:49.198
challenges. That means allowing for
different ways of thinking, learning and

113
00:10:49.199 --> 00:10:53.655
taking part. When we look at belonging,
for instance, we can stay open to the

114
00:10:53.656 --> 00:10:58.288
ways a student experiences connection,
rather than expecting it to look the same

115
00:10:58.289 --> 00:11:02.804
for everyone. In a secondary classroom,
it's easy for the things that need our

116
00:11:02.805 --> 00:11:07.262
immediate attention to fill the whole
picture. A difficult transition. A task

117
00:11:07.263 --> 00:11:11.484
that hasn't started. A moment when joining
the group has become too much.

118
00:11:11.485 --> 00:11:15.648
Those things matter. We need to understand
them and provide support. And

119
00:11:15.649 --> 00:11:20.282
we need to keep the rest of the person
visible. What interests them? What brings

120
00:11:20.283 --> 00:11:24.915
them joy? How do they connect? What can
they do that we might miss if we're only

121
00:11:24.916 --> 00:11:29.607
watching for difficulty? Take one of your
students now. What becomes more visible

122
00:11:29.608 --> 00:11:34.064
when you include their strengths and
interests in the picture? Just hold that

123
00:11:34.065 --> 00:11:35.296
thought for a moment.

124
00:11:43.320 --> 00:11:48.561
A strengths-based approach also takes
difficulty seriously. A student can have

125
00:11:48.562 --> 00:11:54.008
real strengths and still need substantial
support. Their capacity can change with

126
00:11:54.009 --> 00:11:59.591
the task, the environment and the day. So
our language needs to make room for both.

127
00:11:59.592 --> 00:12:04.356
We can describe what's becoming difficult,
notice what the situation is

128
00:12:04.357 --> 00:12:09.599
asking of the student, and consider what
support would help. We begin with the

129
00:12:09.600 --> 00:12:12.390
person, and we stay curious about access.

130
00:12:13.920 --> 00:12:18.579
The social model helps us put that
curiosity to work. On this slide, we're

131
00:12:18.580 --> 00:12:23.686
using an environment-first lens. We look
for barriers in the place, the task, the

132
00:12:23.687 --> 00:12:28.538
routine and the expectations. Then we ask
what can change around the student.

133
00:12:28.539 --> 00:12:33.453
Here's an imaginary classroom example. A
class is gathering on the floor for a

134
00:12:33.454 --> 00:12:38.368
story. The usual expectation is that
everyone sits close together, in the same

135
00:12:38.369 --> 00:12:43.539
space, for the whole reading. One student
finds that arrangement difficult. Before

136
00:12:43.540 --> 00:12:48.263
deciding what the student needs to do
differently, we can look at what this

137
00:12:48.264 --> 00:12:53.434
routine asks of them. How crowded is the
space? What choices are there about where

138
00:12:53.435 --> 00:12:58.030
to sit? Is sitting in that particular
place necessary to take part in the

139
00:12:58.031 --> 00:13:03.201
story? We might explore another place to
sit, or a different way of joining. Those

140
00:13:03.202 --> 00:13:08.436
are possibilities to investigate with the
student. We haven't established what will

141
00:13:08.437 --> 00:13:13.351
help just by coming up with an option. The
next two steps on the slide matter:

142
00:13:13.352 --> 00:13:18.458
include the student's communication and
experience, and review whether the change

143
00:13:18.459 --> 00:13:23.565
makes participation more workable. What
can the student tell or show us about the

144
00:13:23.566 --> 00:13:28.480
change? Is it helping them take part?
Changing an environment won't make every

145
00:13:28.481 --> 00:13:33.140
difficulty disappear. It gives us
somewhere useful to begin, with barriers

146
00:13:33.141 --> 00:13:36.396
and supports that we can notice, discuss
and review.

147
00:13:37.920 --> 00:13:42.365
Let's widen that question to school
itself. You can use the space on page

148
00:13:42.366 --> 00:13:47.119
five of your Participant Guide as you
think. What is school for? Who is school

149
00:13:47.120 --> 00:13:51.441
for? How do students learn? And whose ways
of communicating, moving and

150
00:13:51.442 --> 00:13:56.257
participating are treated as expected?
These are big questions. There isn't one

151
00:13:56.258 --> 00:14:01.135
correct answer we're trying to arrive at.
They're an invitation to look again at

152
00:14:01.136 --> 00:14:05.704
familiar routines and the assumptions
inside them. Think about a routine in

153
00:14:05.705 --> 00:14:10.582
your classroom: perhaps arriving in the
morning, joining a group or showing what

154
00:14:10.583 --> 00:14:15.213
you've learnt. What does that routine seem
to assume about how students will

155
00:14:15.214 --> 00:14:19.721
communicate or participate? What do we
notice and encourage? What might be

156
00:14:19.722 --> 00:14:24.352
harder to see? This is a chance to examine
the routine with curiosity. Every

157
00:14:24.353 --> 00:14:29.229
classroom has patterns that have become
familiar. Looking at them again can open

158
00:14:29.230 --> 00:14:33.984
up choices. We can value the care and
effort that educators bring, while still

159
00:14:33.985 --> 00:14:38.430
asking whether a familiar expectation is
helping every student access the

160
00:14:38.431 --> 00:14:42.813
learning. For a moment, work quietly on
your own. Choose one of the four

161
00:14:42.814 --> 00:14:47.383
questions that catches your attention, and
jot down a thought or a question

162
00:14:47.384 --> 00:14:48.740
you'd like to explore.

163
00:14:56.760 --> 00:15:01.453
Hold on to the question you've chosen. Our
assumptions influence what we notice,

164
00:15:01.454 --> 00:15:05.849
what we reward and what we think needs to
change. We'll now take that wider

165
00:15:05.850 --> 00:15:10.424
reflection into one specific routine, so
the question becomes something we can

166
00:15:10.425 --> 00:15:11.434
examine together.

167
00:15:12.960 --> 00:15:15.520
Now turn to a small group near you.

168
00:15:15.520 --> 00:15:19.160
To begin, take the question about whose
ways of communicating, moving

169
00:15:19.160 --> 00:15:19.960
and participating

170
00:15:19.960 --> 00:15:22.640
are treated as expected.

171
00:15:22.640 --> 00:15:26.700
Choose one familiar routine or decision to
explore together.

172
00:15:26.700 --> 00:15:29.080
Keep it specific enough to picture.

173
00:15:29.080 --> 00:15:32.570
You might choose how children join a
whole-class discussion, for

174
00:15:32.570 --> 00:15:33.280
example.

175
00:15:33.280 --> 00:15:36.800
Talk about the routine and the system,
without naming students,

176
00:15:36.800 --> 00:15:38.320
colleagues or schools.

177
00:15:38.320 --> 00:15:41.400
Then work through the prompts on screen.

178
00:15:41.400 --> 00:15:43.700
Which assumptions shape that routine?

179
00:15:43.700 --> 00:15:45.420
Who can access it easily?

180
00:15:45.420 --> 00:15:48.160
Where might the environment create a
barrier?

181
00:15:48.160 --> 00:15:51.360
How could curiosity change the next
response?

182
00:15:51.360 --> 00:15:55.900
If you find yourselves focusing mainly on
what a student does, come

183
00:15:55.900 --> 00:15:57.060
back to what the environment,

184
00:15:57.060 --> 00:16:00.800
task or expectation is contributing.

185
00:16:00.800 --> 00:16:04.210
Before we come back together, choose one
concise observation to share,

186
00:16:04.210 --> 00:16:05.300
and one example of how

187
00:16:05.300 --> 00:16:09.500
curiosity could change your next response.

188
00:16:09.500 --> 00:16:11.980
Keep the prompts in view as you talk.

189
00:16:11.980 --> 00:16:14.500
You have nine minutes for this discussion.

190
00:16:14.500 --> 00:16:18.260
Pause the video while you work, then
resume when you're ready for the

191
00:16:18.260 --> 00:16:19.000
debrief.

192
00:16:19.000 --> 00:16:20.251
You can begin.

193
00:16:28.280 --> 00:16:33.046
Let's come back together. Finish the
thought you're on, and keep your notes.

194
00:16:33.047 --> 00:16:37.813
If you're sharing with the group, offer
one concise observation: one example

195
00:16:37.814 --> 00:16:42.834
where curiosity could change your next
response. Keep the example focused on the

196
00:16:42.835 --> 00:16:47.855
routine, the task or the environment. As
we close, keep that question about your

197
00:16:47.856 --> 00:16:52.622
next response with you. The shift we're
practising is from assumptions about

198
00:16:52.623 --> 00:16:57.198
students towards questions about access,
environment and learning design.

199
00:16:57.199 --> 00:17:01.838
Curiosity gives us time to look at the
task and the setting before placing

200
00:17:01.839 --> 00:17:06.859
another demand on a student. It helps us
identify something to explore, with the

201
00:17:06.860 --> 00:17:11.880
student's experience still at the centre.
Keep your example available as we move

202
00:17:11.881 --> 00:17:12.071
on.

203
00:17:13.600 --> 00:17:17.140
We've been looking at the barriers that
can sit in school routines,

204
00:17:17.140 --> 00:17:18.680
spaces and processes.

205
00:17:18.680 --> 00:17:22.680
There is a legal framework behind this
work as well.

206
00:17:22.680 --> 00:17:25.820
Let's take a moment to look at the three
parts on this slide,

207
00:17:25.820 --> 00:17:30.220
beginning with the Disability
Discrimination Act 1992.

208
00:17:30.220 --> 00:17:33.880
The Act makes it unlawful to treat a
person unfairly because of

209
00:17:33.880 --> 00:17:34.860
disability,

210
00:17:34.860 --> 00:17:36.860
including in education.

211
00:17:36.860 --> 00:17:39.120
It gives us an important starting point as
we think about the

212
00:17:39.120 --> 00:17:40.900
opportunities students have at school

213
00:17:40.900 --> 00:17:43.800
and the barriers they may encounter.

214
00:17:43.800 --> 00:17:50.840
The second part is the Disability
Standards for Education 2005.

215
00:17:50.840 --> 00:17:53.560
Students with disability have the right to
take part in education on

216
00:17:53.560 --> 00:17:54.640
the same basis as

217
00:17:54.640 --> 00:17:56.160
other students.

218
00:17:56.160 --> 00:17:58.480
The words on the same basis matter here.

219
00:17:58.480 --> 00:18:02.140
They mean having opportunities and choices
comparable to those

220
00:18:02.140 --> 00:18:04.520
available to other students.

221
00:18:04.520 --> 00:18:07.530
Keep that idea of opportunity and choice
in mind as we consider access

222
00:18:07.530 --> 00:18:09.400
through the day.

223
00:18:09.400 --> 00:18:13.040
The third part brings us to what this asks
of schools.

224
00:18:13.040 --> 00:18:17.200
Consult the student, or an associate such
as a parent or carer.

225
00:18:17.200 --> 00:18:19.000
Make reasonable adjustments.

226
00:18:19.000 --> 00:18:21.560
Prevent harassment and victimisation.

227
00:18:21.560 --> 00:18:25.220
These are obligations that connect
directly with the everyday work of

228
00:18:25.220 --> 00:18:25.680
access,

229
00:18:25.680 --> 00:18:28.040
participation and inclusion.

230
00:18:28.040 --> 00:18:31.520
Consultation means making space for the
student's perspective,

231
00:18:31.520 --> 00:18:35.080
including through an associate such as a
parent or carer.

232
00:18:35.080 --> 00:18:39.090
As we go through the workshop, keep asking
whose perspective we need to

233
00:18:39.090 --> 00:18:39.840
understand,

234
00:18:39.840 --> 00:18:45.280
and what that could help us notice about a
routine, task or environment.

235
00:18:45.280 --> 00:18:47.800
Every educator has a part in this work.

236
00:18:47.800 --> 00:18:50.920
We will come back to reasonable
adjustments and consultation later in

237
00:18:50.920 --> 00:18:51.320
the day,

238
00:18:51.320 --> 00:18:55.320
including our work on partnership in
Session Five.

239
00:18:55.320 --> 00:18:58.660
For now, keep the link with our
discussion: when we notice a barrier, we

240
00:18:58.660 --> 00:19:00.760
have a reason to look closely

241
00:19:00.760 --> 00:19:02.840
at what can change around the student.

242
00:19:02.840 --> 00:19:05.560
For a detailed question about a particular
school situation,

243
00:19:05.560 --> 00:19:10.040
take it to your principal or the relevant
system contact.

244
00:19:10.040 --> 00:19:13.700
The Department of Education resources on
the next slide are places to

245
00:19:13.700 --> 00:19:16.120
read more about this framework.

246
00:19:17.760 --> 00:19:21.080
Here are three Department of Education
resources you can return to

247
00:19:21.080 --> 00:19:21.840
after the workshop.

248
00:19:21.840 --> 00:19:24.080
The first is Getting started.

249
00:19:24.080 --> 00:19:28.960
The second is Advocating with and for your
child: A workbook for

250
00:19:28.960 --> 00:19:30.060
parents and carers.

251
00:19:30.060 --> 00:19:34.110
That workbook is written for families, so
it helps us understand what

252
00:19:34.110 --> 00:19:37.140
parents and carers may be reading as they
prepare for conversations

253
00:19:37.140 --> 00:19:37.840
with school.

254
00:19:37.840 --> 00:19:41.740
The third is Milestones and transitions.

255
00:19:41.740 --> 00:19:44.340
The links are listed in the facilitator
resources.

256
00:19:44.340 --> 00:19:46.480
Make a note of whichever would be useful
to you.

257
00:19:47.020 --> 00:19:50.350
For detailed or school-specific questions,
use the appropriate program

258
00:19:50.350 --> 00:19:51.340
or local channel.

259
00:19:51.340 --> 00:19:55.840
We'll keep connecting these resources with
access, consultation and

260
00:19:55.840 --> 00:19:57.600
adjustments as the day continues.

261
00:19:59.120 --> 00:20:04.227
Autism can be understood as difference and
as a dynamic disability. Holding both

262
00:20:04.228 --> 00:20:09.140
ideas matters. Recognising difference and
strengths does not mean overlooking

263
00:20:09.141 --> 00:20:13.990
difficulty or the need for substantial
support. Access and support needs can

264
00:20:13.991 --> 00:20:19.033
change with the task, the environment,
relationships and the capacity available

265
00:20:19.034 --> 00:20:24.270
at that time. Something a student can do
in one setting, or on one day, may not be

266
00:20:24.271 --> 00:20:29.377
available in another. We need to look at
what is happening around the student as

267
00:20:29.378 --> 00:20:34.614
well as what the task is asking of them.
That takes us away from a fixed judgement

268
00:20:34.615 --> 00:20:39.463
about what someone can always do. It
invites a more useful question: what is

269
00:20:39.464 --> 00:20:44.312
making participation possible here, and
what support is needed now? Keep the

270
00:20:44.313 --> 00:20:49.161
individual in view. There isn't one
pattern that will explain every Autistic

271
00:20:49.162 --> 00:20:53.881
student's experience. Strengths,
difficulties and fluctuating capacity all

272
00:20:53.882 --> 00:20:58.795
belong in the picture we build with the
student and the people who know them.

273
00:21:00.321 --> 00:21:05.166
Here is where the day is taking us. We'll
develop a neurodiversity affirming

274
00:21:05.167 --> 00:21:09.883
approach, deepen our understanding of
Autistic experience, notice how that

275
00:21:09.884 --> 00:21:14.922
experience meets the school day, consider
accessible learning environments, and

276
00:21:14.923 --> 00:21:19.704
work in partnership. The first two
sessions build our shared understanding.

277
00:21:19.705 --> 00:21:24.937
Then we'll bring that understanding into
the rhythms of a secondary school day and

278
00:21:24.938 --> 00:21:30.106
into practical choices about design and
support. Belonging, capacity and joy stay

279
00:21:30.107 --> 00:21:34.758
with us throughout. The workshop schedule
allows for morning tea from ten

280
00:21:34.759 --> 00:21:39.669
forty-five to eleven, and lunch from
twelve thirty to one. Treat those as the

281
00:21:39.670 --> 00:21:44.192
planned timings and follow the
arrangements for your session. Before we

282
00:21:44.193 --> 00:21:49.231
move on, turn to My starting point on page
five of your Participant Guide. Make

283
00:21:49.232 --> 00:21:54.465
a brief note of where you are beginning: a
question, a hope or an idea you want to

284
00:21:54.466 --> 00:21:59.505
examine. We will return to this in our
final reflection. Take a moment to write

285
00:21:59.506 --> 00:21:59.762
now.

286
00:22:07.800 --> 00:22:12.222
Keep that starting point. You don't have
to resolve it yet. Next we'll explore

287
00:22:12.223 --> 00:22:16.818
sensory experience, thinking and internal
signals, using the students you have in

288
00:22:16.819 --> 00:22:19.920
mind to connect the ideas with your own
school context.
